    bio: "Pius 'Mau' Piailug was born in 1932 on Satawal, a tiny coral atoll in the Caroline Islands of what is now the Federated States of Micronesia. His grandfather Raangipi recognised his potential at birth and began training him almost immediately, initiating him into the ancient Carolinian tradition of palu — master ocean navigators who could cross thousands of miles of open Pacific using only the stars, swells, birds, and currents. By the time Mau was a young man he had completed the gruelling initiation ceremonies that conferred full navigational authority, and he was widely acknowledged as one of the last living masters of the complete traditional system.\n\nIn 1973, Mau was approached by the newly formed Polynesian Voyaging Society, which was building Hōkūleʻa — a reconstruction of an ancient Hawaiian double-hulled voyaging canoe — and desperately needed a navigator trained in traditional methods. His decision to accept was not without controversy: traditional navigational knowledge was considered sacred and secret in Carolinian culture, not to be shared with outsiders. Yet Mau recognised that the knowledge faced extinction and that sharing it was the only way to save it. In 1976, he guided Hōkūleʻa from Hawaii to Tahiti without instruments, using nothing but his senses and his memorised knowledge of the ocean. The voyage shattered the academic consensus that deliberate long-distance Polynesian navigation was impossible.\n\nMau continued to collaborate with the Polynesian Voyaging Society for decades, training a generation of Hawaiian and Pacific navigators in the old methods. His most devoted Hawaiian student, Nainoa Thompson, went on to become the first Hawaiian in centuries to navigate by the stars alone, completing a subsequent Hawaii-to-Tahiti voyage in 1980. Mau\'s teaching was characterised by an insistence on direct, experiential learning: he had no written texts, no charts, and no models beyond the ocean itself. 'The ocean is not something to be afraid of,' he once said. 'The ocean is your home.'\n\nMau Piailug died on July 12, 2010, on his home island of Satawal, at the age of seventy-eight. His legacy is immeasurable. The navigational revival he sparked has grown into a worldwide movement: Hōkūleʻa completed a circumnavigation of the globe in 2017, sailing under traditional wayfinding, and dozens of voyaging canoes across the Pacific now carry the knowledge Mau refused to let die. He is remembered not only as a supreme master of one of the most complex practical arts in human history, but as the man whose generosity of spirit saved it."
